# What an invocation got wrong, named by identifier.
#
# `tools/` had no diagnostic range at all, so the refusals a caller
# actually meets — the ones about the command it typed rather than about
# the protocol it wrote — were the one part of the refusal surface with
# no stable name. These five say what an invocation got wrong, and they
# are deliberately not about the subject: everything the tools refuse
# *after* reaching their subject is a library judgment carrying that
# library's identifier.
#
# Each of these exits 2, because naming the invocation's mistake is the
# same statement as "this never reached its subject"
# (docs/getting-started.md §4a). The identifier says which mistake.

# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t

# E900 — an input that cannot be read, or an output that cannot be
# written. The same identifier for both because they are the same fact
# about the invocation: a path it named does not work.
# RUN: not zkc-registry-lint %t/absent.json 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=READFAIL
# READFAIL: [zkc-E900]
# RUN: not zkc-family %t/absent.json --emit-vocabulary=%t/v.json --emit-spine=%t/s.mlir 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=READFAIL2
# READFAIL2: [zkc-E900] cannot read
# RUN: not zkc-translate --id %S/../Encoding/schnorr.mlir -o %t/nosuchdir/out.txt 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=OUTFAIL
# OUTFAIL: [zkc-E900]

# E901 — the flags name no job, or more than one.
# RUN: not zkc-translate %S/../Encoding/schnorr.mlir 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=NOJOB
# NOJOB: [zkc-E901] exactly one emission flag required
# RUN: not zkc-run %S/../Encoding/schnorr.mlir 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=NOJOB2
# NOJOB2: [zkc-E901] either --vectors, --prove, or --replay-duplex is required

# E902 — something the invocation names is absent from the environment
# it supplied. Distinct from E900: the file was read, and what the
# caller asked for is not in it.
# RUN: not zkc-relation %S/../Encoding/schnorr.mlir --contracts=%zkc-registry-dir/relation-contracts.json --contract=no.such.contract --protocol-vocabulary=%zkc-registry-dir/protocol-vocabulary.json --construction-profile-registry=%zkc-registry-dir/construction-profiles.json 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=ABSENT
# ABSENT: [zkc-E902] no relation contract 'no.such.contract'
# A file that is not the document a flag takes, caught before the tool
# reaches any subject.
# RUN: not zkc-translate --oir-id %S/../Encoding/schnorr.mlir 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=WRONGKIND
# WRONGKIND: [zkc-E903] expected exactly one protocol container

# E903 — a file the invocation supplied is not the document that flag
# takes. Distinct from E902 again: the environment had what was asked
# for; a *payload* file is the wrong shape.
# RUN: echo 'not json at all' > %t/notjson.json
# RUN: not zkc-run --replay-duplex=%t/notjson.json 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=WRONGDOC
# WRONGDOC: [zkc-E903] fixture:
# RUN: echo '{"transcript":[]}' > %t/notranscript.json
# RUN: echo '{}' > %t/empty.json
# RUN: not zkc-run --replay-duplex=%t/empty.json 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=WRONGDOC2
# WRONGDOC2: [zkc-E903] fixture carries no transcript

# E904 names a generator emitting something its own admission refuses.
# It has no case here and carries a coverage exemption instead: the
# family admits what it emits through the same loaders the tools ship,
# so reaching it needs a defect in the generator rather than an input.
# It stays live and fail-closed because that is the defect it exists to
# catch — the same standing as E237.
